ID  - R73f10f2b1f
TI  - Differential decay mechanisms of human-associated Bacteroidales and bacteriophage genetic markers in aquatic environments: Impacts of indigenous microorganisms on DNA stability and fecal pollution assessment.
JO  - Water research
PY  - 2025
AB  - Human-associated Bacteroidales and bacteriophage genetic markers are crucial indicators for identifying the source of fecal pollution, playing a pivotal role in water quality management and public health risk assessment. However, the differential decay characteristics and underlying mechanisms of these two markers under the influence of indigenous microorganisms remain poorly elucidated. This study investigated the influence of indigenous microorganisms on the decay characteristics of human-associated Bacteroidales and bacteriophage genetic markers (BacHum and CPQ_056, respectively). The results showed that BacHum decayed following a first-order decay model (decay rate k = 2.929/d), while CPQ_056 demonstrated a biphasic decay model (k1 = 2.179/d, k2 = 0.353/d). A two-factor analysis showed that indigenous microorganisms were the dominant factor influencing the decay of both markers, contributing 57-68 % to the overall decay process. To further understand the mechanisms behind the decay differences, propidium monoazide combined with quantitative PCR (PMA-qPCR) was used to examine the dynamic changes of intracellular DNA (inDNA) and extracellular DNA (exDNA) of Bacteroidales and bacteriophages. Indigenous microorganisms contributed approximately 72-81 % to the decay of the two inDNA markers, while exDNA markers exhibited higher stability compared to inDNA, with indigenous microorganisms contributing only 3-43 %. Whether it was inDNA or exDNA, the decay rate of BacHum (inDNA, k = 3.045/d; exDNA, k = 2.866/d) was significantly higher than that of CPQ_056 (inDNA, k1=2.789/d; exDNA, k1=1.425/d) (p < 0.05). These results indicated that the decay of BacHum predominantly relied on the stability of inDNA, while the decay of CPQ_056 was affected by the stability of inDNA during the first phase and exDNA during the second phase of the biphasic decay. Furthermore, the dynamic variation of the concentration ratio of CPQ_056 and BacHum was highly correlated with water quality parameters such as dissolved oxygen, chemical oxygen demand, and turbidity. A ratio of approximately two could be used as the critical point of fresh and aged fecal pollution. This study provided new insights into the decay characteristics and applications of human-associated markers, facilitating a more precise identification of pollution sources and assessment of water pollution.
DO  - 10.1016/j.watres.2025.124157
